From dcca4fe68d6758167b79a8a5a5b81207473ff33e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sirjan Hansda <sirjan.20031231@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:01:21 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] NVRMFB: publish the scanout to the host through ivshmem
 (nvscan)

With nvscan=1 and a QEMU ivshmem device, NVRMFB copies every flipped
frame into the shared memory: a one-page header and two 32 MiB slots.
QEMU's nvscan-display device (or tools/qemu-scanout/nvscan-viewer)
shows it, so a passed-through GPU's output appears on the host without
a capture card.

- The GPU copies the frames: NVRM's copy-engine page-off (nvSurfPageoff)
  writes the flipped surface straight to the slot's guest-physical pages.
  A CPU copy reads VRAM through BAR1, which KVM maps uncached: ~70 MB/s,
  115 ms a 1080p frame inside the flip. The GPU copy takes 0.8 ms.
  The CPU copy stays as the fallback; a timing line every 120 frames.
- The ivshmem device's memory decoding is enabled: no macOS driver
  claims it, so its BAR was assigned but writes went nowhere.
- Flip ABI v3 carries the surface's kernel address (sourceKva) for the
  CPU fallback.
- build/fb_build.sh builds NVRMFB alone; accel_build.sh tolerates a
  missing RM compile_cmds.sh.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017YK5S8VabduTJdwQ14fPhS
